<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siret</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Siret is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suceava">Suceava</a> County, north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and on the river of the same name. In the fourteenth century it was one of the first centres of the Principality of Moldavia: a Catholic bishopric was founded there around 1371, and princes resided there before the court moved to Suceava in the time of Petru I.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siret.jpg" alt="Siret" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Bărbuță · CC BY-SA 3.0 ro · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fosta_prim%C4%83rie_a_ora%C8%99ului_Siret.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Between 1211 and 1225 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> built a fortress on a hill near Siret. The town and the castle were destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> in 1241. According to some historical sources the first document naming Siret dates from 1339, and the town appears as a Russian city in Wallachia in the List of Russian Cities, Far and Near, compiled between 1370 and 1390. In the late fourteenth century it was the capital of the Principality of Moldavia.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> missionaries were welcomed early: a monastery was founded at Siret in 1340. Prince Lațcu, who ruled from 1365 to 1373, promised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> the conversion of himself and his people and asked Pope Urban V to found a Latin diocese in the capital. It was erected in 1371, at first directly subject to the Holy See and from 1412 a suffragan of the Archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a>. It declined from 1388, when Prince Petru moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suceava">Suceava</a>, and from about 1418 the Holy See erected a new Moldavian bishopric at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baia-romania">Baia</a>, which inherited its territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Siret lies at the north-eastern edge of Suceava County, on the right bank of the Siret River and 2 kilometres from the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. It is one of the main border crossings in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, and its railway changes from standard gauge to broad gauge, so that Siret is one of the few places in Romania with equipment to change the gauge of wagons. It had 6,708 inhabitants at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and most are Romanian Orthodox. A Jewish community, recorded by the mid-sixteenth century, made up 14 per cent of the population in 1930; in June 1941 its members were deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transnistria">Transnistria</a>, where more than 700 died.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siret” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus, writing about 950, described Patzinakia as stretching west to the <b>Siret</b> River and divided into eight provinces under the same number of great princes, four tribes on each side of the Dnieper, reflecting the Turkic division into left and right.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pechenegs">Pechenegs · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>His successors, Lațcu and Petru I Mușat, built the institutions of a state: Lațcu sought a Catholic bishopric at <b>Siret</b> around 1371, while Petru I struck coins, did homage to Władysław II Jagiełło of Poland at Lviv in 1387 to balance Hungary, and established the court at Suceava.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Through colonisation and acculturation the formative Pre-Cucuteni/Trypillia A culture took shape, and during the 5th millennium BCE it spread from its homeland between the Prut and the <b>Siret</b> along the eastern foothills of the Carpathians to the plains of the Dnieper and Southern Bug.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cucuteni-trypillia-culture">Cucuteni–Trypillia culture · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
